Let G be a torsionfree group Then:
1. Kn(ZG) = 0 for n ≤ −1;
2. The reduced projective class group K̃0(ZG) vanishes;
3. The Whitehead group Wh(G) vanishes;
4. For any homomorphism w: G → {±1} the w-twisted L-theoretic assembly map
Hn(BG; wL⟨−∞⟩) ∼=→ L⟨−∞⟩n(RG,w) is bijective.

artifact.self_contained_problem:
Let $G$ be a torsionfree (discrete) group, and let $\mathbb{Z}G$ denote its integral group ring. Let $K_n(\mathbb{Z}G)$ denote the algebraic $K$-groups of $\mathbb{Z}G$, let $\widetilde{K}_0(\mathbb{Z}G)$ denote the reduced projective class group, and let $\mathrm{Wh}(G) = K_1(\mathbb{Z}G)/\{\pm g : g\in G\}$ denote the Whitehead group of $G$. For a ring with involution $R$ and a homomorphism $w\colon G\to\{\pm 1\}$, let $L^{\langle -\infty\rangle}_n(RG, w)$ denote the ultimate lower quadratic $L$-groups of $RG$ with the $w$-twisted involution, and let $BG$ be the classifying space of $G$. Show that the following four assertions all hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $K_n(\mathbb{Z}G) = 0$ for every integer $n\le -1$;
\item $\widetilde{K}_0(\mathbb{Z}G) = 0$;
\item $\mathrm{Wh}(G) = 0$;
\item for every homomorphism $w\colon G \to \{\pm 1\}$ and every integer $n$, the $w$-twisted $L$-theoretic assembly map
\[ H_n\bigl(BG;\, {}_w\mathbf{L}^{\langle -\infty\rangle}\bigr) \longrightarrow L^{\langle -\infty\rangle}_n(\mathbb{Z}G, w) \]
is an isomorphism.
\end{enumerate}

